 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, you should stay indoors in Kuala Lumpur today.** \n\nBecause you have asthma, you are in a high-risk group for respiratory triggers, and both the air quality and weather conditions in KL are unfavorable.\n\n**1. Unhealthy Air Quality**\n* **Current AQI:** The Air Quality Index (AQI) across Kuala Lumpur is in the **\"Unhealthy\" range (160–176)**. \n* **Primary Pollutant:** Fine particulate matter (PM2.5) levels are high. These microscopic particles can easily bypass your body's natural defenses, penetrate deep into the lungs, and trigger asthma attacks or severe airway inflammation.\n\n**2. Weather Conditions**\n* **Heat & Humidity:** It is around 30°C–32°C (86°F–90°F) with high humidity (70%–80%), which can make the air feel heavy and harder to breathe.\n* **Afternoon Thunderstorms:** Scattered thunderstorms are forecasted from 2:00 PM to 6:00 PM. While heavy rain can sometimes help wash particulate matter out of the air, the sudden shifts in barometric pressure, wind, and spike in humidity right before a storm can also trigger asthma symptoms (sometimes referred to as \"thunderstorm asthma\").\n\n**Precautions for Today:**\n* **Stay Inside:** Avoid all outdoor physical exertion and stay indoors as much as possible.\n* **Keep the Bad Air Out:** Keep your windows and doors closed. If you have an air conditioner, run it on the recirculation mode, and turn on a HEPA air purifier if you have one.\n* **Mask Up if Going Out:** If you have no choice but to go outside, wear a well-fitted **N95 or KN95 mask**. Standard surgical or cloth masks will not filter out fine PM2.5 particles.\n* **Keep Medication Handy:** Ensure your rescue inhaler (e.g., Ventolin/albuterol) is within reach at all times, and take your preventative controller medications as prescribed.",
